facts:
A square bracket, $[$ or $]$, **includes** the endpoint, the same as $\leq$ or $\geq$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[very thick] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0);
\node[below] at (-1.6,-0.18) {$a$};
\node[below] at (1.6,-0.18) {$b$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-1.6,0) {$[$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (1.6,0) {$]$};
\node[above] at (0,0.3) {$a\leq x\leq b$};
\node[above] at (0,1.2) {$[a,b]$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A round bracket, $($ or $)$, **excludes** the endpoint, the same as $<$ or $>$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-3.4,0) -- (3.4,0);
\draw[very thick] (-1.6,0) -- (1.6,0);
\node[below] at (-1.6,-0.18) {$a$};
\node[below] at (1.6,-0.18) {$b$};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (-1.6,0) {$($};
\node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (1.6,0) {$)$};
\node[above] at (0,0.3) {$a<x<b$};
\node[above] at (0,1.2) {$(a,b)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
